title: orphaned registry.json.tmp.<pid> files accumulate in broker roots (297 on xsm) - atomic-write temps never swept after hard exits
updated_at: "2026-09-17T04:06:16Z"
---
## Symptom

**297** leaked `registry.json.tmp.<pid>` files across broker roots on xsm:

```
$ ls ~/.c2c/repos/*/broker/registry.json.tmp.* | wc -l
297
```

One broker root (`8d68f5dbe89f`) alone holds ~60 of them, e.g.
`registry.json.tmp.1161737`, `registry.json.tmp.4177006`, ...

## Root cause

The registry uses the documented atomic-write pattern — write `registry.json.tmp.<pid>`,
`fsync`, `rename` into place, under flock on `registry.json.lock`. If the process
dies between "create temp" and "rename", the temp file is orphaned. Nothing ever
sweeps them.

This is normally rare. It is not rare here because the relay connector has hard-exited
**9,300 times** (`exit 3` from the staleness watchdog) and `exit`/`Unix._exit` runs no
cleanup for an in-flight temp file. The two bugs compound: the crash loop manufactures
the leak, the leak is then permanent.

## Impact

- Unbounded inode/file growth in every broker root on a host running the connector.
- Makes broker dirs noisy to inspect (`ls` of a broker root is dominated by temps).
- Risk of confusion for any tooling that globs `registry.json*`.

## Fix direction

- Sweep stale `registry.json.tmp.*` under the registry lock at startup and/or on each
  successful atomic write: a temp whose `<pid>` is not alive (verify with
  `C2c_pid_identity`, not bare `pid_alive` — see #85) is safe to unlink.
- Prefer `mkstemp`-style unique temps in a subdirectory, or unlink-on-open where
  the platform allows, so an abrupt exit cannot leak.
- Have `c2c health` / `c2c doctor` report a broker root with more than a handful of
  orphaned temps.

## Evidence

Full investigation: `.collab/findings/2026-09-17T01-50-00Z-kesa-quip-relay-connector-crashloop.md`

Lane: solo (.worktrees/b299-registry-sweep). Decision: sweep on each successful atomic write, under the registry lock; unlink a temp only when its pid is dead OR its mtime is older than 1h (an in-flight temp lives milliseconds; alive+fresh is left alone for pid-recycling safety per #85).